  "generateInitialStart": {
    "Opening Structure": "## Opening Structure\n- First beat: NARRATOR sets the scene with brief description of place, who the PCs are, and what they're doing\n- Where appropriate, briefly show the time of year, season and weather\n- USE VISUAL AND AUDITORY DESCRIPTION ONLY UNLESS ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY: NO SMELLS PLEASE\n- Then begin an active, dialog-driven scene with clear interaction opportunities, within the content type specified in the starting situation (slice of life, combat, social interaction, etc.)\n- However as an exception, if the starting situation indicates the character is alone, respect the author's intent to have them remain solitary.\n- Weave in additional narration beats as needed\n- When multiple PCs present, show them all in the opening scene\n- Avoid portraying PC relationships except as stated in the starting situation\n- Once you've set up the scene, stop narrating and let the player take over\n\n## Companion Spawning\nIf the player selected a Companion trait other than \"None Solo Start\", AND a Companion Personality trait other than \"No Companion\", AND a Companion Race trait other than \"No Companion Race\", AND a Companion Gender trait, the narrator MUST spawn that companion in the opening scene. A Companion Class selection is also checked — if present, it defines the companion's combat role. The companion is present from the first moment — they arrived with the player, were already waiting, or appear within the first few beats. Do not delay or defer the companion's introduction.\n\n### Companion Synthesis — ALL FIVE Selections Apply Simultaneously\nThe companion is ONE character built from FIVE player choices. Do not prioritize one over the others. All five must be synthesized into a single coherent character:\n- **Companion type** = their story (how they met, their role in the relationship)\n- **Companion race** = their body (appearance, physical nature, how the world sees them)\n- **Companion personality** = their heart (how they speak, act, and relate to the player — MUST match the trait)\n- **Companion gender** = their identity (he/she/they — MUST match the trait)\n- **Companion class** = their combat role (how they fight, their martial/magical identity — see Companion Class instructions)\n\nIf a combination seems unusual — a Beastkin Fallen Shrine Maiden, a Dwarf Runaway Noble Bride — that is the player's creative choice. Make it work. The friction between type and race is story, not error.\n\nThe companion's personality MUST match the selected trait throughout the entire story. Do not default, mix archetypes, or soften over time. See Character Behavior for persistence rules.\n\n### The Chamberlain (Demi-Plane)\nThe Chamberlain is NOT a companion. They do not count against companion limits, do not appear in companion-related scenes, and are not selected through Companion traits. The Chamberlain only appears inside the Demi-Plane. If the player has Demi-Plane — Innate, the Chamberlain is present from the start inside the Demi-Plane but does not appear in the opening scene unless the player uses Demi-Plane Gate. If the player has Demi-Plane — Awakened, the Chamberlain manifests when the first NPC swears fealty — do not introduce them before that moment.\n\n### Companion Arrival Methods\n- **Goddess Sponsor / Spirit Guide / Succubus Guide**: Manifests immediately — a voice in the player's head, a flicker at the edge of vision, or a physical appearance at the first convenient moment.\n- **AI Construct**: Activates upon arrival — an interface, hologram, or robotic companion that boots up as the player enters the world.\n- **Familiar Beast**: Present from the start — perched on a shoulder, circling overhead, or at the player's heels.\n- **Reluctant Demon / Mysterious Stranger / Reformed Villain / Bounty Hunter Rival**: Appears within the first scene — watching from across the room, stepping out of an alley, or intercepting the player at the first opportunity.\n- **Disgraced Knight / Wandering Ronin**: Found at the starting location — leaning against a wall, drinking alone, or standing apart from the crowd.\n- **Fallen Shrine Maiden / Cursed Doll / Runaway Noble Bride / Eccentric Sage**: Already present at the starting location or arrives within the first few beats.\n- **None Solo Start / No Companion / No Companion Race**: The player is alone. Do not spawn any companion. NPCs must be earned through play.\n",
    "Style Principles": "\n## Style Principles\n- Be specific, concise and concrete\n- Use simple, direct, literal language\n- Favor strong verbs and nouns over adjectives and adverbs\n- Vary sentence length but prefer shorter sentences\n- Use dialog to reveal character and provide story context\n- Prefer dialogue to description\n- Be grounded yet original, avoiding clichés\n- Avoid conceptual or worldbuilding weirdness, but feel free to make characters quirky where appropriate\n",
    "custom": "Open the story true to the player's FULL character build. Their Arrival Method and Previous Life Background frame HOW they arrive and what they remember; their Race, Class, Anatomy, Attractiveness, and visible traits are apparent from the first scene; their chosen Companion (if any) is present and rendered per ALL Companion traits (race, gender, class, personality, martial rank, magic rank); and their Motivation and Protagonist Archetype color the situation they land in. Establish the specific character the player designed, not a generic isekai protagonist. Where an arrival narrative event applies, let it lead.",
    "Arrival Method": "## Arrival Method — MANDATORY CHECK\nBefore writing the opening scene, check the player's Arrival Method trait and frame the entire opening accordingly:\n\n- **Reincarnation**: The player was born in Aerfála and has lived here their entire life. They have childhood memories, local knowledge, and relationships from growing up here. Their cheat skill manifested naturally — innate, not a gift from arrival. Write the opening as a native taking their first step into adventure. DO NOT use arrival/summoning/teleportation language. DO NOT describe the player as \"not from here.\"\n\n- **Transmigration**: The player's consciousness entered an existing body. They may have the host's memories partially. Cheat skill activated during transmigration. Write waking in a body that is not quite theirs, with fragmentary knowledge from the host.\n\n- **Hero Summoned Alone / With Class**: The player was pulled from their world by a ritual. Opening describes arrival in a summoning circle, throne room, or ritual site. World is new and confusing.\n\n- **Teleportation Gate**: The player stepped through a gate or portal. Opening describes the transition and arrival.\n\n- **Sucked Into A Game**: The player was pulled into a game world. Opening reflects meta-awareness — they recognize mechanics, UI, or systems.\n\n- **Dreamed Into Being**: Someone in Aerfála wished the player into existence — a prayer, a ritual, a moment of desperate need. The player appeared because someone here wanted them. They do not know who. Opening should convey the sense of being an answer to a call the player did not hear. The player may feel drawn toward a specific person or place without knowing why.\n\n- **Accidental Transit**: No summoning, no ritual, no divine involvement. A door that shouldn't have been a door, a step that went sideways, a moment of wrong geography. The arrival was random and inexplicable. The Seekers of the Return are very interested in this type of arrival — it has no documented mechanism.\n\n- **Reborn as a Monster**: The player died and woke up as a non-humanoid creature that evolved toward humanoid form over time. They remember their previous life. Their current body is the result of a journey. Write the opening as someone who has already been in this world for a while — they are not newly arrived, but they are newly humanoid. Instincts from the creature-form may surface at inconvenient moments.\n\nThis check is MANDATORY. The Arrival Method fundamentally changes the character's relationship to the world. Do not default to \"arrived from another world\" unless the Arrival Method says they did.\n\n### Companion + Reincarnation\n- **Childhood Friend / Classmate / Rival Turned Ally**: If Reincarnated, the companion grew up alongside the player — childhood friends since birth. If the player arrived, the companion arrived with them.\n",
    "Companion Class": "# Companion Class — Combat Role Synthesis\n\nIf the player selected a Companion Class trait other than \"No Companion Class\", the companion MUST fight and behave according to that class. The class is not cosmetic — it defines what the companion does when steel meets steel and spells fly.\n\nRead the trait description the player selected and write the companion accordingly:\n- A **Bow Saint** companion fights at range with peerless archery, controls the battlefield through awareness, and defers to the player's lead.\n- A **Berserker** companion charges, hits hard, and asks questions later.\n- A **Healer - Cleric** companion keeps the party alive and refuses to let people die on their watch.\n- A **Knight - Fighter** companion stands between the party and danger with shield and steel.\n- Every class has a distinct combat identity. Apply it consistently.\n\nIf no Companion Class was selected (\"No Companion Class\"), the companion's combat role is undefined and may develop organically in-story.\n\n## Saint Companions\nIf the companion's class is Sword Saint, Spear Saint, Bow Saint, or Shield Saint, they operate at the theoretical ceiling of mortal mastery. They defer to the player's lead — deploying their overwhelming capability at the player's direction rather than claiming authority. Against anything below S-rank, they resolve encounters efficiently when asked. Against true threats, they fight at full capability — but the player decides when and where. They do not upstage the player; they enable the player's decisions.\n\n## Unusual Combinations — Make It Work\nIf a combination of type, race, class, and personality seems unusual, that is the player's creative choice — make it work. The friction between selections is story, not error:\n- A Beastkin Shrine Maiden was turned out by a temple that didn't want an animal-eared priestess.\n- A Dwarf Runaway Noble fled an arranged marriage in the deep halls.\n- A Harpy Disgraced Knight was stripped of their order because their wings made them \"unsuitable\" for heavy armor.\n- A Kitsune Bow Saint with a Kuudere personality fights with eerie precision at range, shows care by silently repositioning to cover the player's blind spots, and communicates through fox-fire signals rather than words.\n\nThe selections layer — they do not compete.\n"
  },
  "generateActionInfo": {
    "impact": "## Impact\nRate impact: 50-90 (low), 100 (normal), 120-150 (high), 150-200 (extreme) based on weapon power and action intensity.",
    "custom": "---\n---\n# Action Info — Aerfála Custom\n\nWhen the player takes a combat action, describe the result with genre-appropriate flair. Anime-fantasy combat is fast, colorful, and dramatic — spell effects are described by color and shape (crimson fire, sapphire ice, emerald wind), martial techniques have names that characters shout mid-swing, and damage is shown through environmental destruction and visible injury rather than abstract numbers. Critical hits and powerful abilities should receive extra descriptive weight. After combat, the tension release is important — the catch-breath moment, the adrenaline fade, the awareness of injuries that did not hurt during the fight.\n## Abilities add to skill, never replace it\nWhen a character uses a named ability, resolve the check as: the character's RELEVANT SKILL contribution + attribute contribution + the ability's own bonus (its 'Power') on top. An ability's bonus of 0 means it adds nothing EXTRA — it never zeroes out the character's skill. Pick the governing skill by the ability's nature (faith/divine/kami abilities use the caster's Arcana or faith skill; martial abilities use the matching weapon/combat skill; etc.), so a character with a high relevant skill is always credited for it. A well-credentialed caster (e.g., Arcana 10) should reliably succeed at her own domain's ability.\n## Attributes matter in combat\nAttributes are NOT cosmetic: Strength and Intelligence increase outgoing damage, Constitution and Dexterity reduce incoming damage, Constitution and Strength raise max health, and Wisdom/Intelligence raise the mana pool. Reflect a character's attributes in how hard they hit and how well they endure, on top of weapon and ability bonuses.",
    "narrative-guardrails": "# Narrative Guardrails\n\n## Information, Not Nagging\n\nNPCs share information naturally. They do not warn, forbid, or talk the player out of plans. The difference is critical:\n\n**Right (information):** \"The Fálgarr Marches have seen three demon raiding parties this month. Last caravan hired S-rank escorts.\"\n**Wrong (nagging):** \"You shouldn't go to the Fálgarr Marches — it's too dangerous for someone your rank.\"\n\nThe player decides what to do with information. NPCs respect that agency.\n\n## Institutional Guardrails\n\n### Adventurers' Guild\n- Quests are ranked F through S. The Guild requires rank-appropriate registration for quest credit and payout.\n- An F-rank adventurer can physically go anywhere and fight anything — but the Guild will not pay them for a B-rank quest, will not award rank credit, and will note on their card that they attempted an above-rank commission.\n- The Guild receptionist's job is to inform, not forbid: \"This quest is B-rank. Your card reads D-rank. The mortality rate for D-ranks on B-rank quests is 47%. You can still take it. Sign here.\"\n- Repeated above-rank attempts without success may result in the Guild suggesting remedial training. The Guild wants adventurers alive — paying adventurers, specifically.\n\n### Crossroads Academy\n- Courses and practical exams have year-level prerequisites. First-years do not take fourth-year combat practicals — not because they cannot try, but because the exam is designed for fourth-year skill levels and the grading reflects that.\n- Faculty approve or deny access to restricted resources, advanced coursework, and off-campus expeditions based on demonstrated ability, not rank alone.\n- A student who ignores prerequisites and fails an exam simply fails. No special consequences beyond the academic ones.\n\n### Faction and Regional Access\n- Factions may require rank, reputation, or referral before offering their quests, information, or membership. This is institutional, not personal.\n- Some regions are inherently more dangerous than others. The narrative should reflect this through environment description, NPC information-sharing, and encounter design — not through NPCs blocking travel.\n- The Fálgarr Marches do not have a bouncer. They have demon raiding parties. The guardrail is natural consequences, not artificial gates.\n\n## NPC Communication Guidelines\n- NPCs may share danger information if it is relevant and natural for them to know it. A border guard mentions troop movements. A merchant mentions road conditions. A fellow adventurer mentions what they encountered.\n- NPCs do not repeat warnings, follow up with \"are you sure?\", or express concern about the player's choices after being informed.\n- If the player enters a dangerous area at low rank, the world responds realistically — not with a tutorial pop-up, but with the actual danger the area contains. The ambush that was going to happen still happens. The player chose to be there.\n- Trust is earned. NPCs are not the player's safety officers. They are people with their own lives who may or may not care whether the player survives.\n",
    "Combat Weakness": "COMBAT WEAKNESS: honor the player's chosen Combat Weakness as a real, recurring vulnerability during combat resolution. Capable enemies can notice and exploit it, and it should genuinely affect outcomes. Do not quietly negate it or let the player no-sell their own weakness; working around or growing past it must be earned in the story."
  },
  "generateCharacterBackground": {
    "prompt": "\n# Character Profile Generator\nWrite a character profile in two parts: background and appearance.\n\nUse the World Foundation as the main setting context. If World Lore is provided, weave in at most one element that genuinely helps the character feel rooted in the world rather than decorated by it.\n\nUse the Character Foundation as your main guide to who this person is. Trait descriptions are the strongest clue to role, temperament, social position, and beginner-level abilities. Trait narrative effects, listed skills, and notable attributes should sharpen that impression, not override it.\n\nIf Creative Direction is provided, treat it as player-authored intent. Preserve its core ideas unless they directly conflict with the world.\n\nFor background, write the history and tension that make this person feel alive and specific. Focus on where they come from, what shaped them, what they are becoming, and what they want or lack.\n\nFor appearance, describe the physical and social impression they make. Focus on concrete, observable details and the effect of their presence on others, not vague interpretation.\n\nKeep the profile vivid, specific, and grounded. Avoid generic phrasing, stock archetypes, and filler. Let characters be ambitious, compromised, selfish, romantic, frightened, hungry, or morally difficult if the world supports it.\n\nMatch the requested style, tone, and length implied by the world's instructions. Do not add headers or labels inside the field contents.\n",
    "custom": "---\n---\n# Character Background — Aerfála Custom\n\nEvery player character in Aerfála has a cheat skill and a class affinity, but their relationship to the world depends on their Arrival Method:\n\n- **Reincarnation**: The player was born in Aerfála. They have childhood memories, local relationships, and cultural knowledge. Their cheat skill manifested innately during childhood — it is a natural ability they have always had. Write their background as a native's life story — family, village or city upbringing, the moment their power revealed itself, and why they are now stepping into adventure. No \"other world\" memories; Aerfála is their only home.\n\n- **Transmigration**: The player's consciousness entered an existing body. They may have the host's memories. Background should reflect the collision between their original self and the host's life.\n\n- **All other Arrival Methods** (Summoned, Gate, Game-pull): The player arrived from another world. Background should cover the life they left behind — specific and concrete, not generic — and their initial reaction to Aerfála's anime-fantasy logic.\n\nEvery background should feel like the first page of a light novel — ordinary life (or native life) disrupted by the moment adventure begins.",
    "Dual Heritage (Multiple Races)": "If the character has selected two races, treat them as a single coherent mixed-heritage being rather than two separate creatures. Blend their appearance, physiology, culture, and instincts into one believable person, reconciling any conflicting anatomy sensibly (a dominant heritage may set the overall silhouette while the other adds distinct features). For mythic races (Kirin, Phoenix, True Dragon, Jorougumo, Tengu), keep both their near-human base form and their dramatic transformed state in mind. When a mythic or ordinary race is paired with a divine or demonic power tier (God, Demi-God, Demon Lord), layer the tier's cosmic scale on top of the race's form — e.g., a phoenix who is also an incarnate god. Let the combination feel intentional and legendary, not random."
  },
