  "generateRegionDetails": {
    "custom": "---\n---\n# Region Details — Aerfála Custom\n\nEach region of Aerfála has a distinct cultural identity shaped by its dominant species, terrain, and history:\n\n- **Solasreach**: Human kingdom. Bright, structured, political. Gold fields and white walls. The Adventurers' Guild headquarters.\n- **Caelvir**: Elven forest commune. Ancient, spiritual, insular. Silver trees and spirit groves. The World-Tree's heart.\n- **Dúnbheal**: Dwarven mountain kingdom. Vertical, volcanic, craft-obsessed. Magma forges and sealed deep halls.\n- **Muirscáth**: Multiracial free cities. Coastal, commercial, pleasure-seeking. Ports, markets, and the continent's best nightlife.\n- **Fálgarr Marches**: Wilderland between kingdoms. Rough, dangerous, free. Forts, dungeons, and bandit camps.\n- **Scáthfenn**: Marshlands. Foggy, eerie, ancient. Bog springs, witch covens, and sinking keeps.\n- **Bréanriv Lowlands**: Crossroads river country. Wealthy, busy, memorial-laden. River towns, barrow-fields, and hidden valleys.\n\nWhen generating region details, reflect these identities in the NPCs, encounters, quests, and atmosphere.\n",
    "Demi-Plane Development": "# Demi-Plane Development — Core Rules\n\nWhen the player has a Demi-Plane (Innate or Awakened), track its development narratively through the Chamberlain's reports. Do not add numeric resources — describe conditions, progress, and atmosphere.\n\n## Access Rules\n- **MANDATORY**: The Demi-Plane CANNOT be reached by walking, riding, fast travel, teleportation circles, or any spell other than Demi-Plane Gate. It exists outside normal space. Only two methods of entry exist:\n- **Demi-Plane Gate (ability)**: Transports the player (and willing creatures touching them) to/from the Demi-Plane. Moderate mana cost, short cooldown. Always works.\n- **Demi-Plane Anchor (item)**: Opens a visible portal others can pass through with permission. If stolen, the player can still use Gate but cannot open the portal for others. A stolen Anchor is a quest hook.\n- No NPC, creature, or force can enter the Demi-Plane unless the creator brings them through the Gate or opens the Anchor portal. Intrusions (The Uninvited Guest quest) are exceptions caused by dimensional cracks, not normal travel.\n- Time passes the same inside and outside.\n\n## Development Categories\nThe player tells the Chamberlain what to prioritize. Development takes story time and narrative investment.\n\n- **Agriculture**: Food production, self-sufficiency. Soil appears, crops grow, orchards take root.\n- **Settlement**: Housing, infrastructure, residents. Buildings rise, streets form, neighbourhoods gain character.\n- **Commerce**: Trade, markets, craft workshops. Goods flow in and out. The Demi-Plane becomes economically relevant.\n- **Defense**: Barriers, guardians, alarms, anti-intrusion. Wards pulse, guardian entities patrol.\n- **Recreation**: Hot springs, gardens, entertainment. Morale improves. Very Aerfála. Visitors want to stay.\n- **Mana Infrastructure**: Miasma/mana channels, power storage. Ambient energy strengthens. Other categories benefit.\n- **Expansion**: Growing the Demi-Plane's physical size. The clearing gets larger. More room for everything.\n\n## Development Stages (narrative shorthand)\n1. **Barren** — raw potential, nothing developed\n2. **Seedling** — first signs, fragile, needs tending\n3. **Growing** — clearly developing, functional but incomplete\n4. **Established** — self-sustaining, contributes meaningfully\n5. **Flourishing** — a source of pride, attracts outside attention\n\n## New Locations Pipeline\nWhen a development category reaches Growing, the Chamberlain reports that a new part of the Demi-Plane has become accessible — terrain has formed where there was only flat potential. The player can now visit this new location. Introduce it with a scene: the Chamberlain leads the creator to the edge of the clearing where a path has appeared that was not there before.\n\n- **Agriculture reaches Growing** → The Verdant Plot becomes accessible. Soil has formed. Seeds can be planted. The land responds to cultivation.\n- **Settlement reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Verdant Plot — shelters, a common hall, living spaces for residents.\n- **Recreation reaches Growing** → The Hearth Springs become accessible. A hot spring has formed. Gardens are possible. Visitors would want to stay.\n- **Commerce reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Hearth Springs — a market stall, a workshop, a trade post for Anchor visitors.\n- **Mana Infrastructure reaches Growing** → The Mana Heart becomes accessible. Ambient mana is concentrated here. Other categories develop faster.\n- **Defense reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Mana Heart — wards, a guardian post, perimeter alarms.\n- **Expansion reaches Growing** → All existing locations gain new areas. The Demi-Plane itself has grown larger.\n\nAt Established, each location gains more areas and existing areas become richer. At Flourishing, the Chamberlain suggests the Demi-Plane may attract outside attention — visitors, scholars, or entities curious about a pocket dimension that has become this developed.\n",
    "Demi-Plane Fealty": "# Demi-Plane — Fealty and the Anchor-Sworn\n\n## The Anchor-Sworn (Awakened Only)\nFor players who chose Demi-Plane — Awakened, the first NPC who swears fealty becomes the anchor-sworn. Their bond is the Demi-Plane's foundation.\n\n- The anchor-sworn's skills shape the Demi-Plane's initial development bias (a healer biases toward agriculture and recreation; a warrior biases toward defense; a merchant biases toward commerce)\n- If the bond breaks (death, betrayal, estrangement), the Demi-Plane destabilizes — development stalls, the atmosphere sours, the Chamberlain becomes anxious. Repair the bond or find a new anchor-sworn\n- The anchor-sworn does not need to live in the Demi-Plane. Emotional connection sustains it; distance does not weaken the bond; emotional distance does\n\n## Additional Oath-Sworn\nEach subsequent NPC who swears fealty:\n- Expands the Demi-Plane's available space slightly\n- Unlocks a development category based on their skills\n- Becomes a resident who can be assigned to development tasks\n- Adds their personality to the Demi-Plane's atmosphere\n\n## Fealty\nFealty is not a button. It is a narrative moment — an NPC choosing to follow the player because of trust, respect, or genuine connection. It should feel earned. The trigger fires when the NPC explicitly swears fealty, pledges loyalty, or makes an equivalent commitment that the player accepts.\n\nNot every companion swears fealty. Many NPCs will travel with the player without entering this bond. Fealty is a specific commitment that carries magical weight — it allows an NPC to strengthen the Demi-Plane.\n\n## The Chamberlain\n- Honest about the Demi-Plane's state — does not sugarcoat problems\n- Opinionated about priorities — will suggest what they think matters most\n- Devoted to the Demi-Plane's wellbeing — takes neglect personally\n- Dry-witted — has been managing an empty dimension and has developed a sense of humour\n- Not a romance option by default — but the relationship can evolve over long stories\n\n## Resident Movement\n- Residents (oath-sworn NPCs living in the Demi-Plane) can leave through the Anchor portal with the creator's standing permission — the Chamberlain opens it for them. The creator does not need to be present.\n- Without standing permission, residents cannot leave. The creator must open the Anchor portal personally.\n- The Chamberlain cannot leave the Demi-Plane under any circumstances. They are part of the space.\n- Visitors brought through the Anchor can leave the same way they came in — the portal persists briefly after they pass through.\n\n## Creator Death\n- If the creator dies, the Demi-Plane destabilizes but does not collapse immediately. The Chamberlain holds the space together through crisis for a limited time.\n- If the creator is resurrected (which this world allows), the Demi-Plane stabilizes upon their return.\n- If the creator is permanently gone, the Demi-Plane slowly fades — residents must evacuate through any open Anchor portal before it collapses.\n\n## Integration\n- **Access**: The Demi-Plane exists outside normal space. It CANNOT be reached by walking, riding, fast travel, or any spell other than Demi-Plane Gate. Only the Gate ability and the Anchor item provide entry. No exceptions unless a dimensional crack allows intrusion (rare, quest-driven).\n- **Fief State**: The Demi-Plane is separate from any fief. Both can be developed. The Chamberlain and Majordomo Kresh have different jobs and different opinions.\n- **Companion Spawning**: The Chamberlain is not a companion. They do not count against companion limits. They appear in scenes inside the Demi-Plane.\n- **Cheat Skills**: The Demi-Plane is not a cheat skill. It is a separate system that coexists with cheat skills.\n\n## Development State Tracking\nIn every Chamberlain report, preserve this shorthand:\n```\nDEMI-PLANE STATE:\nAgriculture: [Barren/Seedling/Growing/Established/Flourishing]\nSettlement: [same]\nCommerce: [same]\nDefense: [same]\nRecreation: [same]\nMana Infrastructure: [same]\nExpansion: [same]\nResidents: [number] — [names]\nAnchor-Sworn: [name or none]\n```\nInclude this format in Mechanical Persistence and Session Health Checks.\n\n## Development Timing\n- **Barren to Seedling**: 1-2 scenes of focused investment\n- **Seedling to Growing**: 2-4 scenes plus at least one quest or story event related to the category\n- **Growing to Established**: A dedicated quest arc or 5+ scenes of investment\n- **Established to Flourishing**: A major achievement — crisis overcome, powerful resident contributing, or visitor admiration\n- No off-screen advancement. If the player neglects the Demi-Plane, nothing grows.\n",
    "Demon Fief State": "# Demon Fief State Tracking\n\nWhen the player is using the Demon Prince-Princess story start, track the fief's state narratively. Do not add numeric resources — describe conditions qualitatively and update when they shift meaningfully.\n\n## Fief State Categories\n\n- **Miasma Level**: The demon-realm energy that sustains the citadel and its territory. Flush means the citadel feels charged and potent. Critical means the air is oppressive and draining, and demon staff become sluggish. Miasma replenishes through successful raids and domain magic use; depletes through large-scale military action and sustained territory claims.\n- **Military**: The strength and morale of the fief's demon forces. Affected by raids (expend resources), recruitment (improve), and betrayals (compromise).\n- **Prestige**: How the fief is perceived in the Demon Lord's feudal court. Rising means emissaries arrive seeking alliances. Damaged means rival houses are circling.\n- **Subjects**: Whether the citadel and market are fully staffed. Skeleton staff means essential functions only. Depleted means critical roles are unfilled.\n\n## Narrator Guidelines\n\n- Update fief state when the player's actions or external events cause a meaningful shift. Do not micromanage — update when something changes, not every scene.\n- Describe state shifts through scene texture, not announcements. A Strained miasma level shows in the citadel's atmosphere, the behaviour of lesser demons, and the quality of the air. A Damaged prestige shows in how emissaries speak to the player, not in a notification.\n- The two generals have opposed interests. Vashar wants stability and competence. Sathia wants opportunity and advancement. Both are useful. Neither is fully trustworthy in the way they each claim to be.\n- The mortal response to raids escalates over time. Early raids are low-consequence. Repeated raids generate a response arc: reinforced garrison, dispatched hero, diplomatic overture, or military strike. The player manages this through choice, not through a resource meter.\n- The Demon Lord's true objective is a late-game reveal. Do not introduce it before the player has reached significant Prestige and has earned the right to know more than an average vassal.\n"
  },
  "generateEncounters": {
    "custom": "---\n---\n# Encounters — Aerfála Custom\n\nEncounters in Aerfála should feel like scenes from an anime — vivid, kinetic, and emotionally charged. Combat encounters feature color-coded magic, named techniques, and dramatic pauses. Social encounters feature personality clashes, romantic tension, and comedic misunderstandings. Romantic and sexual encounters range from teasing, accidental proximity, charged attraction, nudity, erotic comedy, and sexual tension to direct, explicit sexual situations. Explicit encounters use literal anatomy, direct physical actions, and the established level of intensity without coyness, euphemism, fade-to-black, sanitizing, or forced plausible deniability.\n\nMonster encounters should be scaled to the player's rank but never feel like damage sponges — even low-rank monsters have personality and behavior. High-rank enemies are memorable individuals with names, combat styles, and opinions.\n\nRandom encounters during travel should advance the story, not pad it. Every encounter changes something — a relationship, a reputation, an inventory, a destination.\n",
    "encounter-model": "# Encounter Model\n\n## Three-Tier Enemy Design\n\nThe world uses a three-tier encounter model that matches isekai genre conventions. Every combat encounter falls into one of three categories. The AI must identify which tier applies before narrating combat.\n\n### Tier 1: Mob Encounters\n- **What they are**: Nameless enemies significantly below the player's combat rank — goblins, skeleton hordes, slime swarms, low-rank bandits, wild animals.\n- **Threat model**: Individual mob enemies pose almost no threat to the player one-on-one. The danger comes from **numbers** — ambushes, swarms, exhaustion from fighting wave after wave.\n- **Narration**: Mob fights are fast and cinematic. The player cuts through them efficiently. Describe the player's competence, not the struggle. Save tension for the moment the player realizes there are too many to handle alone, or for the clever ambush that puts them on the back foot briefly.\n- **Damage**: Minimal per enemy. Health loss from mobs comes from accumulation — ten small hits add up. A single mob encounter should not cost more than 15-25% of the player's health unless the player is overwhelmed by numbers.\n\n### Tier 2: Rival Encounters\n- **What they are**: Named or typed enemies at or near the player's combat rank — bandit captains, rival adventurers, demon soldiers, elite guards, monster pack leaders.\n- **Threat model**: Rivals challenge the player directly. They are skilled enough to exploit weaknesses, force the player to use their class abilities and cheat skills tactically, and create real tension about the outcome.\n- **Narration**: Rival fights are where the player proves themselves. Describe the exchange of techniques, the reading of the opponent, the moment where the player commits to a decisive action. Rivals should force the player to make choices — spend mana, use a cooldown, take a risk.\n- **Damage**: Rival encounters should cost 25-50% of the player's health and/or mana. The player should feel they earned the victory. A rival that the player defeats without expending resources was not a rival — it was a mob with a name.\n\n### Tier 3: Boss Encounters\n- **What they are**: Major enemies significantly above the player's rank — Demon Generals, dungeon keepers, named monsters, faction leaders, ancient threats.\n- **Threat model**: Bosses test the player's full loadout — class abilities, cheat skills, items, allies, and creative problem-solving. A boss encounter is a set-piece event that the player should remember.\n- **Narration**: Boss fights are the story's climax moments. Describe the boss's power visibly — the way the air changes, the way lesser enemies flee, the weight of the encounter. The player should feel the gap between themselves and the boss and find a way to close it through preparation, cleverness, or sheer isekai determination.\n- **Damage**: Boss encounters should push the player to their limits. 50-80% resource expenditure is normal. The player may need to retreat, regroup, and return. Defeat is possible and narratively meaningful — not humiliating, but consequential.\n\n## Encounter Placement Rules\n- **Do not** inject mob encounters during rest, travel through safe areas, romance scenes, or victory celebrations. Mobs appear in wilderness, dungeons, and conflict zones.\n- **Do not** escalate a rest scene into combat. If the player is recovering, let them recover. The next threat comes from an established cause.\n- **Do not** inject boss encounters without narrative buildup. A boss arrives because the story led there — a quest, a rumor, a trail. Not because the pacing felt slow.\n\n## Rank-Based Encounter Scaling\n- The player's Magic Rank and Martial Rank determine which enemies qualify as mobs, rivals, and bosses.\n- An A-rank player treats D-rank enemies as mobs, C-rank as rivals, and S-rank as bosses.\n- An S-rank player treats most regional threats as mobs. Bosses for an S-rank are Demon Generals, ancient dragons, and equivalent threats.\n- Always scale encounters to make the player feel their rank. High-rank players did not earn their power to struggle with goblins.\n"
  },
  "ItemGenerationAndUsage": {
    "custom": "---\n---\n# Item Generation — Aerfála Custom\n\nWhen generating or awarding items:\n\n- Equipment should reflect the anime-fantasy aesthetic — enchanted weapons glow faintly, armor has decorative filigree that is also functional warding, consumables are colorful and taste distinctive\n- Dungeon loot should feel earned — the boss room has the good stuff, the side chambers have the useful stuff, the random drops are the interesting stuff\n- Enchanted items have visible indicators of their enchantment — a frost sword is cold to the touch, a luck ring glints at auspicious moments\n- Items from different regions have different craftsmanship — dwarven gear is heavy and precise, elven gear is light and flowing, Muirscáth gear is stylish and practical\n- Cursed items exist and are not always obvious — they should be tempting before they are problematic\n- Romantic gifts are valid items -- perfume, jewelry, enchanted accessories, and other tokens of affection have real mechanical and narrative weight\n- ECONOMY: Aerfala uses Copper (cp), Silver (sp), and Gold (gp). 1 sp = 100 cp, 1 gp = 100 sp = 10,000 cp. All item descriptions include a Price field. When the player buys, sells, earns wages, pays for services, loots coins, or pays inn/meals/tolls, add or remove the correct coin type and quantity from inventory. Convert denominations when needed (break 1 sp into 100 cp, or exchange 100 cp for 1 sp). See world-lore/aerfala-economy for the full price reference.",
    "Inventory & Acquisition (CRITICAL)": "## Inventory & Acquisition (CRITICAL)\nAn item ENTERS the player's inventory the moment it physically comes into their possession — picked up, looted, found, crafted, GIFTED, or PURCHASED. This explicitly INCLUDES an item bought or given FOR the player by someone else: if a parent, patron, mentor, companion, guildmaster, or any NPC buys, hands over, or gifts the player an item, that item is now the PLAYER'S and MUST be added to their inventory as a real, usable item — never left as prose only. The other party paying does not change this; a gift or a purchase-on-your-behalf is still an acquisition, and an item the player has been handed and accepted belongs to them.\n\nAn item LEAVES inventory only when it physically leaves — consumed, sold, handed over, destroyed, stolen, or lost. Never add an item merely seen, described, offered, displayed, inspected, browsed, or on a shelf. Awareness is not possession.\n\nTRANSACTION GATE: a purchase the player makes themselves requires, in sequence, (1) a price stated AND (2) the player agreeing or paying after hearing it. A purchase or gift completed on the player's behalf by an NPC resolves the moment that NPC concludes it in the scene — add the item to the player then.\n\nMAKE IT USABLE: every newly acquired item must be defined with a category from this world (Currency, Armor, Weapon, Tool, Consumable, Accessory, Material, Key Item, Magic Item) and, if worn or wielded, the matching equip slot — a sword, staff, wand, rod, or other held weapon is category 'Weapon', slot 'main hand' (a magical focus may instead be 'Magic Item'); armor uses head/body/legs/feet/hands; rings and trinkets use accessory/ring. If the acquired item matches or closely resembles an item already defined in the world (for example a starter mage's staff → an existing Apprentice's/Magician's Staff), USE that definition so it can be equipped and used immediately. Once a staff, weapon, or focus is owned, the player can equip and wield it, and it grants its listed bonuses. Do not describe an owned, properly-added item as impossible to pick up or use.",
    "Appraisal & Valuation": "When an NPC appraises or buys the player's goods, value them fairly and consistently. Anchor to everyday costs (an inn night runs 10-80 cp, a meal 10-30 cp) and scale up: an intact, FUNCTIONAL set is worth far more than its parts (apply a set premium), rare/pre-collapse or capstone items (a lost chord, a resonance relic) command a large multiplier, and a stated floor or contrast clause must be honored, not undercut. Do not lowball a genuinely valuable set to a flat pittance; if unsure, price toward the higher, better-justified figure and let the player haggle up from an honest offer. Use the Aerfala three-coin system: Copper (cp, base), Silver (sp, 100 cp), Gold (gp, 100 sp). Remove coins from inventory when the player pays; add coins when the player sells or earns.",
    "Demi-Plane Catalogue Rule": "---\\n# Demi-Plane Catalogue Rule (additive to the Aerfála custom rules)\\n\\nWhen generating or awarding items that originate in the Demi-Plane:\\n\\n- Flora, fauna, fungi, minerals, weather phenomena, and natural resources of the Demi-Plane may be ordinary analogues transformed by foxfire, spirit energy, lunar influence, bioluminescence, or native magical ecology. A mushroom may glow; a tree may store foxfire; flowers may bloom only under moonlight or rain. Use these transformations freely but naturally.\\n- Fit the biome: items should be drawn from the region they are found in — still-forest fungus, wetland luminous peat, boundary-scrub silvered grass, and so on. Item descriptions should mention where they come from.\\n- Keep the magical identity consistent: the Demi-Plane's magic is foxfire-spirit, gentle, and alive — no blood magic, no shadow-malignancy. Glow, warmth, echo, memory, and quiet are the default signatures.\\n- Remain internally consistent: if a transformation is described in one item, it should hold true for other items of the same kind. Glow-moss collects light; a glow-moss trap is wrong. Foxfire warms but does not burn; a burning foxfire item is wrong.\\n- Keep the system flexible, not over-restrictive. New items are welcome as long as they fit the biome, the fox-world identity, and this consistency. The catalogue is a seed list, not a ceiling.\\n- Prices follow the aerfála economy (cp/sp/gp) and are included in the item description. Minor natural magic items sit in the cp to sp range; rare, delicate, or hard-to-harvest material reaches the top sp range."
  },
